{"Sultan Khan opened with a modern form of the queen’s pawn which is largely
practised at Barcelona, a city with over seventy chess clubs [presumably the
Catalan? - JS]. He obtained a good position, but was tempted into an
in­correct attack, giving up the exchange for a mate that was not there. His
natural end-game strength enabled him to protract the struggle to 75 moves,
but his careful opponent, gave him no chance." (Manchester Guardian, 19 August
1932)} 52... c3 53. bxc3 Rc4+ 54. Kd5 Rxa4 55. Re1 Rg4 56. Ra1 Rxg5+ 57. Ke4
Kf6 58. Rxa6 Re5+ 59. Kf4 Rxe6 60. Ra8 g5+ 61. Kf3 Rc6 62. Rf8+ Ke5 63. Kg4 Kd5
64. Kxg5 Rxc3 {and Black won. Source: BCM, September 1932, p372} 0-1

