56... Ra3+ ({BCM annotates this "a surprise" but gives no other comment or
variation. But the fairly obvious transition into a K+P ending is a simple win:
} 56... Rc4+ 57. Kd3 Rxc2 58. Kxc2 b4 $1 {or 58...Kc5!, etc.} ({But not} 58...
Ke5 $2 59. Kd3 g5 60. hxg5 hxg5 61. f3 b4 62. Ke3 Kd6 63. f4 {, which is
tenable for White})) ({Even if Black is unable to calculate the K+P ending,} 
56... Rxe4 {seems more logical than the text move, which is only good enough
to draw.}) 57. Kb4 Rf3 58. Kxb5 Ke5 59. Kc4 h5 60. Ra2 (60. Re2 $11) 60... Kxe4
61. Re2+ Kf5 62. Kd4 Kg4 63. Ke4 $2 (63. Kd5 $1 $11) 63... Rf5 $1 64. Ke3 Re5+
65. Kd2 $2 (65. Kd3 $1 $13) 65... Kf3 $19 66. Rxe5 fxe5 67. Ke1 e4 68. Kf1 e3
69. fxe3 Kxg3 {Source: BCM, September 1932, p375, but the names of the players
were mistakenly given as Yates White and Tylor Black. The Times, 20 Aug, 1932,
makes it clear that the colours were Tylor White, Yates Black (as does the
crosstable subsequently published in BCM).} 0-1

