1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 d5 4. Bg5 Be7 5. e3 O-O 6. Nf3 Nbd7 7. Rc1 c6 8. Qc2
c5 9. cxd5 exd5 10. Be2 b6 11. O-O Bb7 12. dxc5 Nxc5 {Presumably part of
Black's plan is to bring this knight to e4 later on, and he had quite a good
position at this stage.} (12... Bxc5 {was the alternative and this had its
bearing on White playing Ne5, but the determining factor was that if Black did
retake with the bishop, then came} 13. Rfd1 {, and Black must lose a move with
the queen, because of the threatened Nxd5.}) 13. Rfd1 Rc8 14. Qb1 h6 15. Bh4 a6
16. Qf5 Re8 ({The previous move was doubtless played to prevent 16.Nb5 but 16.
Qf5 should have warned Black of the danger of leaving the f-pawn without
protection. There certainly was the threat to the d-pawn for Black to consider,
but White would have to play Bxf6 first, to which Black would reply Bxf6; and
then the exchanges could produce equality.} 16... Rc7 {looks to have been more
to the point.}) 17. Ne5 Nce4 18. Nxe4 Rxc1 ({Black had committed himself by
his last move, and now if} 18... Nxe4 19. Qxf7+ Kh8 20. Ng6+ {mate [JS note:
not mate! But it is winning]}) ({nor could Black play} 18... dxe4 {; for then
came} 19. Rxd8 {, so exchanging rooks was the least harmful way out of the
trouble.}) 19. Nxf6+ Bxf6 20. Rxc1 Bc8 ({After this Black could not play} 20...
Rxe5 {as then the reply was} 21. Qxe5 {, coming out an exchange to the good.
Altogether a most interesting combination.}) 21. Bxf6 gxf6 22. Rxc8 {Decisive,
for White comes out a piece to the good.} Qxc8 23. Qxc8 Rxc8 24. Nd3 Rc2 {
Unless this is played, Nf4 might follow; and the disorganised condition of the
black pawns meant that Black could not protect them all. In any case, Black
must lose the d-pawn, and then there was nothing useful left.} 25. Kf1 a5 26.
Ke1 a4 27. Kd1 Rc4 28. Nf4 Rc5 29. Bf3 d4 30. exd4 Rc4 31. Ne2 b5 32. Bd5 Rc8
33. Kd2 b4 34. Kd3 Kf8 35. Bc4 Ke7 36. Nf4 f5 37. Nd5+ Kd6 38. Nxb4 Rg8 39. g3
f4 40. a3 Rg5 {Source: Times Literary Supplement, 6 Oct 1932, p716.} 1-0

